@Font editing
On the font editing screen, you can edit the characters of a font. Select a
character to edit (in the left window) by ALT+arrows, and then use arrows to
move the cursor in the right window and space to toggle the pixel.

!ed;Editing characters
!me;Memory
!ma;Macros
!ba;Batch mode

:ed;Editing characters
You can use arrows to move the cursor in the right window and space to toggle
the pixel. Push delete to clear the current glyph, and you can push insert to
revert it to the PC font.

Push tab to toggle draw mode; when the draw mode is activated, then it will
set every pixel the cursor passes over, so that you do not need to push the
space bar each time.

In addition, there are some functions which modify the entire picture:
  <I> Toggle all pixels
  <F> Flip vertically
  <M> Mirror horizontally

The numbers shown to the right of the character editing window are numeric
representation of the character raster, and can be used in the variable
property menu to modify a single character or in a #EDITFONT command in a
script to modify a single character.

:me;Memory
There is an internal memory that can store a single character glyph; push Y
to store the current glyph in the memory.

You may also store one of nine predefined patterns in memory by using the
numbers 1 to 9; the predefined patterns are as follows:
  ° ± ² Û Ü Ý Þ ß þ
  1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9

You can then alter the current character glyph with the memory by using the
functions mentioned below. The memory will remain even after you go to a
different character to edit, or to a different font to edit, but will be
lost if you quit the Super ZZ Zero editor, or overwrite the memory.

Push A, O, or X to modify the current glyph by performing a AND, OR, or XOR
operation on it with the memory.

Push P to overwrite the current glyph with a copy of the memory.

Push Z to exchange the current glyph with the memory.

:ma;Macros
You can record a macro, which is a sequence of operations; you can record up
to 128 commands in one macro.

Push F1 to start recording, and then enter the commands that you want to be
recorded (they will be executed while doing so) and then F1 again to stop
the recording. You can push F2 at a later time to play back the macro; it
can be played back multiple times.

However, the following commands cannot be recorded:

þ Exiting the font editor

þ Switching to batch mode (doing so will cancel the recording)

þ Any operations in batch mode

þ Starting/stopping recording and playing back macros

Macros are also usable in batch mode; see below. (You cannot record macros
in batch mode but you can play back macros.)

:ba;Batch mode
The batch mode allows you to perform operations on multiple characters at
once. Push B to enter or leave batch mode.

Use the arrows to move the cursor, and space to mark which characters you
want to be selected for batch operations. If you hold the control key while
moving the cursor, then it will mark the characters that the cursor passes
over, so that you do not need to push space bar for each one.

You can also push U to unselect all characters, or N to select all of the
unselected characters and vice-versa.

The operations A, F, I, M, O, P, and X will operate like in the normal mode
but affects all selected characters, instead of the current one.

If you push F2 in the batch mode, it will apply the macro to each of the
selected characters. The macro is allowed to move to other characters to
be edited, which will be affected whether or not they have been selected
for batch operation; however, the macro should then go back to the
originally selected character before stopping recording the macro. (In this
way, it is possible to copy several characters at once, possibly with
modifications such as being flipped and/or mirrored.)

